### What are some common traits among the elements in an adjacency matrix representation of a bipartite graph? ###

In an adjacency matrix representation of a bipartite graph, each element in the matrix has a unique row and column index that corresponds to the vertex in either the left or right part of the bipartition. This uniquely determines the edge connecting the two vertices in the graph. The matrix is further constrained to only have entries in the left-hand column and bottom-row that are 0 because the left and right parts of the bipartition are not connected. The 0 entries indicate the absence of an edge connecting the two vertices. Other useful properties of an adjacency matrix representation of a bipartite graph are that all edges are explicitly represented and it is easy to locate all the edges incident to a given vertex.

